# Reverse wireless charging ("power share") -- arcfox / motorola razr 50 ultra.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS DEVICE CAN TRANSMIT (measured, not assumed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The wireless charging front end is a ConvenientPower CPS4041 -- stock ships
#    its firmware as vendor/firmware/cps4041.bin and cps4041_2nd.bin, both
#    exactly 32768 bytes, which is the part's 32 kB MTP. The CPS4041 is a
#    single-chip Qi RECEIVER *and* TRANSMITTER (60 W Rx / 20 W Tx). Motorola
#    rates power share on this model at 5 W.
# 2. The kernel side is already live on OUR build. Measured on the running
#    device (adb, 2026-09-01):
#      /sys/devices/platform/soc/soc:qcom,pmic_glink/
#          soc:qcom,pmic_glink:qcom,battery_charger/
#      contains tx_mode, tx_mode_vout, rx_connected, rx_dev_id, rx_dev_type,
#      rx_dev_manufacturing, wlc_tx_capability, wlc_tx_id, wlc_tx_power,
#      wlc_tx_sn, wlc_tx_type, wlc_st_changed, wls_input_current_limit.
#    Those files are created by qti_glink_charger (kernel/motorola/
#    sm8635-modules/motorola/drivers/power/qti_glink_charger/
#    qti_glink_charger_core.c:2784 wireless_psy_init(), called unconditionally
#    from probe at :3673) on chg->wls_psy->dev.parent -- which is exactly the
#    directory /sys/class/power_supply/wireless/device resolves to. The driver
#    is bound: it also registers the "main_battery" supply, and
#    /sys/class/power_supply/main_battery points at soc:qcom,pmic_glink:
#    mmi,qti-glink-charger on this device.
# 3. Stock's product partition declares the feature:
#    product/etc/permissions/com.motorola.hardware.wireless_power_share.xml.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE NODE CONTRACT (read from stock's own HAL, not guessed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Disassembly of stock /vendor/bin/hw/motorola.hardware.wireless.powershare-
# service, symbol MotPowerShare::setWlsTxMode(TxMode, TxResult*) at 0x60f0
# (symbols recovered from its .gnu_debugdata):
#
#   TxMode == 1 -> WriteStringToFile("1", "/sys/class/power_supply/wireless/
#                                          device/tx_mode", false)
#   TxMode == 0 -> WriteStringToFile("0", <same path>, false)
#   then getIntFromFile(<same path>) and compare the readback.
#
# That is the WHOLE of turning power share on. There is no second write, no
# current-limit setup, no sequencing. tx_mode_store() in the kernel
# (qti_glink_charger_core.c:1789) parses the buffer with kstrtoul(buf, 0, ...)
# -- base 0, so "1" and "0" are unambiguous -- and forwards the value to the
# ADSP charger firmware as OEM_PROP_WLS_TX_MODE. Everything after that is
# firmware; the kernel only relays.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY init AND NOT THE VENDOR HAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The HAL is not the obstacle -- it works, and it does have a setter
# (setWlsTxMode; the AIDL interface hash is 0de1c6377a3c776edb6eed27a2bfee8d268-
# 963d2 and the NDK lib exports Bp/Bn for it). The obstacle is that it has no
# CLIENT here. On stock the chain is
#   CoreSettingsExt.apk  ->  motorola.core_services.power.MotoPowerManager
#   (moto-core_services.jar)  ->  com.android.server.power.powershare.
#   WirelessPowerShareService (Motorola's patched services.jar)  ->  the HAL.
# All three of those are Motorola-proprietary and this ROM ships none of them.
# Rebuilding a Java client would mean reconstructing the .aidl -- method ORDER
# (transaction codes) and the layout of the Result/TxResult/TxMode/TxStatus
# parcelables -- from a stripped binary, with no way to validate a wrong guess
# except by flashing. Since the HAL's entire contribution to *enabling* TX is
# one 1-byte write to a node init can write directly, that risk buys nothing.
#
# What the HAL would additionally buy is STATUS: it polls rx_connected and
# reports peer device id/type/manufacturer through a callback. This file does
# not reproduce that, and the toggle above it reports the REQUESTED state, not
# the measured one. Read the trailing note in the app for what that costs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXACT PROPERTY NAME, AND THE ONE SEPOLICY LINE IT NEEDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The trigger has to be settable at runtime by an app, and readable by
# vendor_init. Those two constraints together pick the property almost by
# themselves:
#
#  * NOT a vendor property. A coredomain is neverallowed from setting any
#    non-system property (private/property.te, the compatible_property_only
#    block), and system_app is a coredomain -- so ro.vendor.* / vendor.* /
#    persist.vendor.* are unreachable from an app, exactly as recorded in
#    init.arcfox-touch-gesture.rc.
#
#  * The trigger is CHECKED AT PARSE TIME. system/core/init/action_parser.cpp
#    IsActionableProperty(): for an action in a /vendor rc file the subcontext
#    is non-null, and this device has ro.actionable_compatible_property.enabled
#    = true (measured with getprop), so unless the name starts with one of the
#    partner prefixes (ro.vendor./vendor./persist.vendor./odm./ro.boot./...)
#    init calls CanReadProperty("u:r:vendor_init:s0", name) and REJECTS the
#    whole action with "unexported property trigger found" if vendor_init
#    cannot read it. That is why the touch-gesture file can key off
#    sys.boot_completed: it is boot_status_prop, and private/vendor_init.te:322
#    grants get_prop(vendor_init, boot_status_prop).
#
#    Plain "sys." maps to system_prop, which vendor_init CANNOT read -- so a
#    bare sys.* name would silently disable this file.
#
#  * exported_system_prop satisfies both sides with no new type and no new allow
#    rule: it is system_public_prop (public/property.te:230), every domain gets
#    the read via get_prop(domain, exported_system_prop) (private/domain.te),
#    and private/system_app.te:45 already grants
#    set_prop(system_app, exported_system_prop). The compatible_property_only
#    neverallow that guards it exempts coredomain and appdomain, so system_app
#    is clear.
#
# THE ONE LINE THIS FILE DEPENDS ON, in
# device/motorola/sm8635-common/sepolicy/system_ext/private/property_contexts:
#
#     sys.arcfox.powershare           u:object_r:exported_system_prop:s0
#
# Without it the name falls through to system_prop; the app can still SET it
# (set_prop(system_app, system_prop)), but init refuses to parse the two actions
# below and the toggle does nothing. That is a safe failure -- init logs the
# rejection and carries on -- but the feature is dead until the line lands.
#
# Deliberately NOT persist.*: transmitting must not silently survive a reboot,
# and persist.* values are restored from /data long after boot anyway.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO NEW SELINUX RULE IS NEEDED FOR THE WRITE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These commands run in vendor_init (this file installs to /vendor/etc/init),
# and AOSP grants
#   allow vendor_init { sysfs_type -sysfs_usermodehelper }:file rw_file_perms;
# (system/sepolicy/private/vendor_init.te:235), plus sysfs_type:dir r_dir_perms
# and sysfs_type:lnk_file read at :233-234 -- which covers walking the two
# symlinks in the path (/sys/class/power_supply/wireless, and .../device).
# tx_mode is plain u:object_r:sysfs:s0 in this tree (stock labels it
# sysfs_wls_dev; we do not declare that type -- see sepolicy/vendor/
# init_wls.te). Either label is in the sysfs_type attribute, so the rule holds
# either way.
#
# This MUST stay a /vendor rc file. `neverallow init sysfs:file { open write }`
# (private/init.te:862) applies to the init domain proper; only the vendor_init
# subcontext, which is what vendor rc commands execute in, may write sysfs.
#
# DAC: tx_mode is 0644 root:root as created by the driver. stock's
# /vendor/bin/init.mmi.wls.sh (which this tree ships and starts from
# init.mmi.overlay.rc:235 on sys.boot_completed) chmods it 0660 and chowns it
# system:system so that the HAL -- which runs as user system -- can write it.
# init is root either way, so this write works before or after that script.

on property:sys.arcfox.powershare=1
    write /sys/class/power_supply/wireless/device/tx_mode 1

on property:sys.arcfox.powershare=0
    write /sys/class/power_supply/wireless/device/tx_mode 0
